bezny is a React component framework built on a shared design system with CSS custom properties.

## Installation

```bash
pnpm add @bezny/button @bezny/styles
```

## Setup

Import the default theme CSS in your application entry point:

```css
@import "@bezny/styles/theme.css";
@import "@bezny/button/index.css";
```

To create a custom theme instead, see the [Theming](/theming/overview/) guide.

## Usage

```tsx
import { Button } from "@bezny/button";

function App() {
  return <Button variant="primary">Click me</Button>;
}
```

bezny uses a three-tier CSS custom property system for theming. A single SCSS mixin generates all tokens, media query responses, and class overrides from a few base colors. No build step is required for dynamic theming at runtime — everything runs on CSS custom properties and `oklch()`.

## Three-tier token architecture

### Tier 1: Primitive tokens

Raw color scales derived from base colors. Each palette produces 11 shades (50 through 950) using oklch lightness interpolation.

```css
--bzn-primary-50:  oklch(97% ...);
--bzn-primary-100: oklch(93% ...);
/* ... */
--bzn-primary-950: oklch(15% ...);
```

Five palettes are generated: `primary`, `neutral`, `success`, `warning`, and `danger`.

### Tier 2: Semantic tokens

Role-based aliases that reference primitives. This is the only layer that changes between dark and light modes.

```css
--bzn-text:              var(--bzn-neutral-50);
--bzn-surface:           var(--bzn-neutral-950);
--bzn-interactive:       var(--bzn-primary-600);
--bzn-border:            var(--bzn-neutral-700);
--bzn-focus-ring:        var(--bzn-primary-400);
--bzn-error:             var(--bzn-danger-500);
```

Semantic tokens cover text, surfaces, borders, interactive states, focus, feedback (success/warning/error), motion, and transparency.

### Tier 3: Component tokens

Per-component variables that default to semantic tokens. Components only reference this tier.

```css
--button-primary-bg:     var(--bzn-interactive);
--card-bg:               var(--bzn-surface-raised);
--input-border:          var(--bzn-border);
```

Component tokens never reference primitives directly — they always go through semantic tokens.

## How the tiers connect

```
Primitive (oklch shades)
  -> Semantic (roles: text, surface, interactive, ...)
    -> Component (button-bg, card-border, input-color, ...)
```

Overriding at any tier cascades down. Change a primitive and every semantic token referencing it updates. Change a semantic token and every component using it follows. Override a component token to target a single widget.

## Default theme

bezny ships a default theme using bordeaux (primary) and slate (neutral) palettes. Dark mode is the default, with automatic light mode via `prefers-color-scheme`.

To use the default theme, import the compiled CSS:

```css
@import "@bezny/styles/theme.css";
```

For custom themes, see the [Custom Themes](/theming/custom-themes/) guide.

Create a custom theme by using the `bzn.theme()` SCSS mixin. It generates all three token tiers plus media query responses from your base colors.

## Simple API

Pass oklch colors and the mixin generates full shade ramps automatically:

```scss
@use '@bezny/styles/theme' as bzn;

:root {
  @include bzn.theme(
    $primary: (h: 250, c: 0.18),
    $neutral: (h: 250, c: 0.01),
  );
}

@include bzn.responsive();
```

The `h` (hue) and `c` (chroma) values are used with a fixed lightness curve to produce 11 shades per palette. Chroma is automatically reduced at the extremes (50, 900, 950) to avoid oversaturation.

## Full API

For precise control, pass complete shade maps instead of hue/chroma pairs:

```scss
@use '@bezny/styles/theme' as bzn;

$my-brand: (
  50:  oklch(97% 0.02 280),
  100: oklch(93% 0.05 280),
  200: oklch(85% 0.08 280),
  300: oklch(73% 0.13 280),
  400: oklch(62% 0.17 280),
  500: oklch(50% 0.2 280),
  600: oklch(45% 0.2 280),
  700: oklch(38% 0.18 280),
  800: oklch(30% 0.15 280),
  900: oklch(23% 0.12 280),
  950: oklch(15% 0.08 280),
);

:root {
  @include bzn.theme(
    $primary: $my-brand,
    $neutral: bzn.$slate,
  );
}

@include bzn.responsive();
```

You can mix approaches: use a shade map for one palette and hue/chroma for another.

## Mixin parameters

| Parameter | Default | Description |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| `$primary` | `$bordeaux` | Primary brand palette (shade map or `(h, c)`) |
| `$neutral` | `$slate` | Neutral/gray palette |
| `$success` | `$emerald` | Success feedback palette |
| `$warning` | `$amber` | Warning feedback palette |
| `$danger` | `$rose` | Danger/error feedback palette |
| `$transition` | `200ms` | Default transition duration |
| `$prefix` | `'bzn'` | CSS variable prefix |

## Built-in palettes

The following palettes are available as SCSS maps from `@bezny/styles/palettes`:

- `$slate` — neutral gray-blue
- `$bordeaux` — warm red (default primary)
- `$emerald` — green (default success)
- `$amber` — yellow-orange (default warning)
- `$rose` — pink-red (default danger)

```scss
@use '@bezny/styles/palettes' as palettes;

:root {
  @include bzn.theme(
    $neutral: palettes.$slate,
  );
}
```

## Responsive mixin

The `bzn.responsive()` mixin must be called at the root level (outside `:root`) to emit media queries and class overrides. Without it, color scheme switching and accessibility preferences will not work.

```scss
@use '@bezny/styles/theme' as bzn;

:root {
  @include bzn.theme($primary: (h: 250, c: 0.18));
}

// This must be at the top level, not inside :root
@include bzn.responsive();
```

## Overriding individual tokens

After calling the theme mixin, you can override tokens at any tier:

```scss
:root {
  @include bzn.theme(
    $primary: (h: 250, c: 0.18),
    $neutral: (h: 250, c: 0.01),
  );

  // Override a semantic token (affects all components using it)
  --bzn-surface-raised: var(--bzn-neutral-800);

  // Override a single component token
  --button-primary-bg: var(--bzn-primary-700);
}
```

You can also override tokens in plain CSS without SCSS:

```css
:root {
  --bzn-interactive: var(--bzn-primary-700);
  --card-bg: var(--bzn-neutral-900);
}
```
